WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.300
A neuron can receive an inhibitory input that moves its membrane potential by 0 mV.

2
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.580
It can still stop the cell from firing.

3
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.460
How? The usual picture says inputs are voltages that add and subtract.

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.020
That picture cannot explain this one.

5
00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:22.543
Think of each input as a conductance instead.

6
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:27.763
When a synapse opens channels, it adds conductance, measured in nanosiemens.

7
00:00:28.443 --> 00:00:32.703
Those channels have a reversal potential, the voltage at which current through them

8
00:00:32.703 --> 00:00:37.723
is zero. Every open conductance pulls the membrane toward its own reversal

9
00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:38.723
potential.

10
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:42.277
Here is a made-up cell, with the same voltage everywhere.

11
00:00:42.837 --> 00:00:44.157
All values are illustrative.

12
00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:49.717
At rest, the leak conductance is 10 nS, reversing at minus −65 mV.

13
00:00:50.357 --> 00:00:51.657
Threshold is minus −50.

14
00:00:52.237 --> 00:00:56.437
Now open an excitatory conductance of 5 nS, reversing at 0.

15
00:00:57.097 --> 00:01:00.377
The voltage settles at −43.3 mV.

16
00:01:00.777 --> 00:01:04.877
The voltage is an average of the reversal potentials, weighted by conductance.

17
00:01:05.237 --> 00:01:06.337
That is above threshold.

18
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.700
Now add an inhibitory conductance of 10 nS.

19
00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.200
Its reversal potential equals rest.

20
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.720
The average now includes a second pull toward rest.

21
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.020
The voltage settles at minus −52 mV.

22
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.940
That is below threshold, so there is no spike.

23
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:28.160
Take the excitation away, and the inhibition alone leaves the voltage at minus −65.

24
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.440
The trace does not move, yet it vetoed the spike.

25
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.610
Inhibition like this is called shunting.

26
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:39.350
It lowers the input resistance, so the same excitatory current makes a smaller

27
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:45.150
voltage change. It also shortens the time constant, from 20 ms to 10 Give it two

28
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.670
idealized instant pulses, 10 ms apart, each worth 2 mV.

29
00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.170
Without the shunt, they add up to 3.21 mV.

30
00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.730
With the shunt, only 2.74.

31
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.330
Here the shunt did not shrink each jump.

32
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.890
It left less of the first pulse for the second to ride on.

33
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:07.813
So an inhibitory input is defined by what it does to firing, not by which way the

34
00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:10.693
trace moves. Inputs add as conductances.

35
00:02:11.453 --> 00:02:14.553
Each one pulls the voltage toward its own reversal potential.

36
00:02:15.313 --> 00:02:18.473
A shunt can leave the voltage alone and still veto the spike.
